# Copy to .env.local (gitignored) for local dev, or set as build-time env vars
# in the hosting dashboard for production. Both are read at build time via
# import.meta.env — see src/analytics.ts.

# PostHog project API token. Analytics stay fully disabled (no-op) if unset.
VITE_POSTHOG_KEY=

# Where posthog-js sends events. This file carries the PRODUCTION value, because
# copying it into a hosting dashboard is the obvious thing to do — and the
# failure mode of getting it wrong is invisible (see below).
#
# `/edge` is the same-origin reverse proxy served by worker/index.ts, which
# forwards to PostHog so ad-blockers don't drop events before they leave the
# browser. It only works on a deploy that includes the Worker.
#
# For local dev and forks, where there is no Worker in front, use the direct
# host instead:
#
#   VITE_POSTHOG_HOST=https://us.i.posthog.com
#
# Careful: that direct host is ALSO the code-level fallback in analytics.ts, so
# setting it explicitly and leaving the var unset are the same thing — both
# silently give up the proxy while events keep flowing. It reads like a valid
# production config and isn't. Verify after deploy with the checklist in
# docs/design/analytics.md.
#
# Prefer the bare path below over an absolute `https://openshaper.com/edge`. An
# absolute URL stops being same-origin as soon as a page is served from anywhere
# else — `http://`, a `www.` host, a preview deploy — and posthog-js's requests
# are then rejected by CORS, losing those visits entirely. `resolveApiHost` in
# analytics.ts repairs an absolute value at runtime, but a path never breaks in
# the first place.
VITE_POSTHOG_HOST=/edge
